  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nTonight through next Wednesday...Moisture will subtly increase \nacross the region as southerly flow at the surface and aloft has \nreturned.  Rain chances will increase further on Friday, with good \nchances continuing through the upcoming weekend, before backing down \nduring the first part of next week. The best rain chances through \nthis forecast period will again come Saturday and Sunday. This is \nwhen the presence of a weakening trough will be most observed across \nthe forecast area, as ridging aloft flattens and gets nudged more to \nthe east of the Lower Mississippi River Valley. This setup, coupled \nwith added ascent from the sub-tropical jet just to our north, and \ndaytime heating creating an unstable airmass, will yield mainly \nafternoon showers and storms developing across the forecast area, \nwith some activity lingering into the evening hours each day.\n\nAt the moment, these storms are not expected to be severe. However, \nsome gusty winds can't be ruled out with the more intense \nthunderstorms. Due to the very dry soil conditions across the area \nand overall rainfall being spread out over multiple days, widespread \nflash flooding isn't expected. However, localized heavy downpours in \na short amount of time and the potential for some training of \nconvection, could result in the ponding of water on roads and in low-\nlying and poor drainage areas, again especially Saturday and Sunday.\n\nRain chances will begin to decrease from northwest to southeast \nMonday into mid-week.  A cold front will shift southeast through the \nCWA Monday.  Cool dry high pressure will build into the forecast \narea in this front's wake through mid-week.  Currently while rain \nchances will come to an end across much of the area, some isolated \nshowers will remain possible across mainly Southeast Mississippi in \nthe vicinity of the front as it stalls across the north central \ngulf.  This scenario will result in cooler drier conditions across \nthe forecast area as we begin the new work week. /19/",
